Zeroing instruction-token attention at L1 across all 32 heads does not affect instruction classification ($100\%$ maintained via the residual stream) but \emph{increases} action MLP probe $R^2$ from $0.256$ to $0.375$ ($\Delta = +0.119$, 95\% CI $[0.065, 0.167]$, $p < 0.0001$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), indicating that instruction-dominant heads redirect attention from image to instruction tokens at the cost of local action encoding.
Deep-layer representations are unaffected ($|\Delta R^2| < 0.01$ at L8 and L13, 95\% CIs spanning zero, $p > 0.1$), confirming that the attention redistribution is confined to L1.
The restriction of this effect to L1, with L8 and L13 unchanged, argues against a probe-level artifact from reduced input dimensionality.

