\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Synthesis.}
Image tokens remain instruction-blind across all 32 layers, consistent with the causal mask and frozen encoder; the readout token serves as the sole multimodal convergence point.
VLA fine-tuning shifts instruction routing from mid-depth (L9--L16) to an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1, L31), with training effects concentrated at the readout token ($R^2$ $+0.069$) rather than image representations ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$).

\paragraph{Mechanism-behavior connection.}
The instruction-blindness of image tokens provides a mechanistic explanation for the visual dominance reported by Grant et al.~\cite{grant2026}: the action pathway relies on the readout token for task conditioning.
Under RSA, DINOv2 achieves the highest geometric alignment ($\rho = 0.692$), yet VLA readout surpasses it in decodability ($R^2 = 0.678$ vs.\ $0.634$), indicating that the LLM integrates instruction-conditioned information rather than enhancing visual geometry.

\paragraph{Architectural implications.}
Attention ablation at L1 confirms functional specialization: removing instruction attention does not disrupt classification but redistributes attention to image tokens, recovering action encoding ($R^2$ $+46\%$, $p < 0.0001$), with deep layers unaffected ($|\Delta R^2| < 0.01$).
This dissociation confirms genuine head specialization while cautioning against interpreting attention as a causal bottleneck.

\paragraph{Limitations and scope.}
We study OpenVLA-7B on LIBERO; generalization to other VLAs (e.g., RT-2~\cite{brohan2023_rt2}, $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024_pi0}) and task domains remains open.
Token-level specialization generalizes via the causal mask to any decoder-only VLA; the routing pattern may be Llama-2-specific.
The encoder ranking (DINOv2 $>$ DinoSigLIP $>$ CLIP under RSA) reverses under CKA (Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}), indicating metric dependence; we caution against strong conclusions from RSA alone.
Because OpenVLA uses a frozen encoder, the DINOv2 advantage reflects pre-fusion geometry within a fixed configuration; end-to-end encoder fine-tuning may change this ordering.
The L1 ablation uses 5 demonstrations per task; a full-scale replication is in progress.
All analyses are post-hoc; metric sensitivity appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:metric_sensitivity_appendix}.
In sum, we verify token-level specialization, reveal front-loaded routing as the primary learned contribution, and characterize non-monotonic geometry under RSA; code will be released upon acceptance.

